  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe showers continue to exit the Berkshires and NW CT early this\nafternoon with the wave of low pressure and the mid level\ntrough. The warm front never made it through and will wash out,\nas a cold front quickly approaches from the west. Cloud cover is\nabundant now, but has begun to thin over the western Dacks and\nwestern Mohawk Valley. The best SBCAPE on the SPC RAP\nMesoanalysis is over these areas presently in the 500-1000 \nJ/kg range. The latest 12Z HREFs continues to the show mean \nSBCAPEs 750-1250 J/kg mainly west of western New England late \nthis afternoon into early tonight with lesser amounts over \nwestern New England in the 300-750 J/kg range. The clouds/lower\nstratus will have to clear quickly for destabilization to occur\nby the mid to late pm over eastern NY/western New England. The \n0-6 km shear is 35-40 KT and slightly stronger upstream of the \nforecast area. Mid level lapse rates are relatively weak to \nmarginal into tonight in the 5-6.5 C/km. However, a strong mid \nand upper level jet streak is approaching western NY and \nsoutheast Ontario by 00Z/Mon. Low-level moisture is still in \nplace with dewpoints in the 60s and PWATS ranging 1.3-1.8\" \nacross the region. The cold front will be the focus for any \nthunderstorms. The CAMs continue to struggle with the coverage \nand extent of any strong to potentially severe thunderstorms. \nHowever, the latest 3-km HRRR/3-km NAM/WRF-ARW do have some \ndiscrete organized cells form into a cluster/broken line and \napproach the western Mohawk Valley/southwest Dacks and Schoharie\nValley towards 23Z/7 pm and 01Z/9 pm and then weaken towards \nthe Greater Capital Region/Lake George Saratoga Region/Taconics \nprior to midnight likely due to waning/lessening instability \nsupporting taller updrafts. \n\nSPC continues the Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for most of the\narea north and west of the Mid-Hudson Valley/southern\nTaconics/NW CT and did upgrade a small area of the western\nMohawk Valley (southern Herkimer/western Montgomery/northwest \nSchoharie) to Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5). Damaging winds would \nbe the main threat especially with stronger winds aloft and the \nDCAPE values on some of the CAMS in these areas 750-1000 J/kg.\nIsolate severe hail is also possible in the Slight Risk area. The\nscattered showers and isolated to scattered thunderstorms will \ndiminish between midnight to 3 am or so from northwest to \nsoutheast across the forecast area. It will become cool and \nbreezy behind the front. Lows fall back into the 50s, except \nsome mid and upper 40s over the southern Dacks, southern Greens \nand northern Catskills. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nStrong cold advection occurs in the wake of the front through\nMonday morning. Deeper mixing and momentum transfer will allow \nfor northwest winds 10-15 mph with some gusts 20-30 mph. The \nNBM/NBM90 winds/wind gusts were used for Monday. Temps were \nlowered slightly from the NBM closer to MAVMOS guidance with max\ntemps running about 5 degrees or so below normal with upper 60s\nto lower 70s in the valleys and upper 50s to mid 60s over the \nhigher terrain. The winds should diminish quickly in the late pm\nand early evening, as a sfc anticyclone moves over upstate NY \nand western New England. Clear skies and calm winds will promote\nideal radiational cooling conditions with lows falling into the\n30s and 40s. The mid and upper 30s will be mainly over the \nsouthern Dacks, upper Hudson Valley, southern Greens and \nnorthern Catskills, where patchy to localized areas of frost \nare possible. A frost advisory may be needed for Hamilton and \nnorthern Herkimer counties, if coverage and confidence increases\nfor Mon Night/Tue morning. \n\nSlightly below normal to seasonable temps and dry weather \ncontinues on Tuesday with the sfc high slowly retreating. Temps\nrise to near to slightly above normal readings by Wed. A cold \nfront brings the next chance of showers and a few thunderstorms \nWed night into Thu across eastern NY and western New England.",
